export const WRITING_INDEX = "1. \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026) /2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html [NEWEST]\n   A white paper on what a developer's job really looks like inside a large enterprise in the age of AI, across twelve areas from planning and requirements to governance and production.\n2. \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026) /2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html\n   Auto model routing in today's coding tools mostly manages capacity rather than matching the task, and task-aware routing is genuinely hard to build.\n3. \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026) /2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html\n   Five years ago nobody believed AI would arrive this fast. It did. I think quantum is the next thing we're underestimating, and the reason is that AI and quantum have started accelerating each other.\n4. \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026) /2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html\n   We can't measure productivity-per-token, so every AI budget today is a guess. Why soft limits are the right interim policy, and why the answer lives at the gateway.\n5. \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026) /2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html\n   Skills, workflows, prompts, MCP servers and CLAUDE.md files are one primitive, injected context, and using it well is the highest-leverage AI skill to learn next.\n6. \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026) /2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html\n   Most agent failures now come from missing context, not weak models: the bottleneck has moved from the AI to the person telling it what they want.";
export const SITE_CHUNKS = [{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"","text":"I'm a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office. I've shipped AI and non-AI platforms end to end, from orchestration and agent tooling to real-time event systems and network analytics, working as architect, developer, DevOps engineer and product owner inside a company where security, compliance, and stakeholder management are not optional.\n\nThe thing I care about is the combination. I live at the front of the AI-tooling curve and use it to build whatever the work needs: early MCP adopter, design partner for AT&T's internal AI coding tool, part of a ~25-person design-partner cohort at Windsurf, and the go-to person for AI-fueled coding across the CDO. But I do that *while* shipping real work at enterprise scale. Most engineers I meet have one side or the other. I'm trying to be good at both, and this site is where I write about what that's taught me.\n\nThis page is the longer version of my [resume](): the work, the projects I build on my own time, and what I'm looking for next.","lead":true},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"At AT&T","text":"I started at AT&T in the Technology Development Program in 2024 and moved into a Senior Data/AI Engineer role in the Chief Data Office in early 2026. The pattern across both has been the same: take on the thing nobody else has untangled yet, ship it end-to-end, then turn it into something the rest of the org can reuse."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Platforms I've built","text":"- **A multi-tenant enterprise platform on Kubernetes.** I'm the technical lead and application architect for a confidential orchestration platform, guiding a ~10-person team of engineers and contractors (most more senior than me) from planning and governance approval through Azure build-out, networking and delivery. We reached a working multi-tenant environment serving multiple organizational groups in about four months, on Helm, Terraform, Entra ID, PostgreSQL, Redis and GitHub Actions, with Grafana, Prometheus, Loki and Tempo for observability and 166 automated test files covering load, penetration and chaos testing. - **A real-time geofence event-orchestration platform.** As primary application developer I took it from first code to production in about five months, then shipped a second release with roughly 10x the capabilities. It coordinates priority connectivity at large live events, and it turned workflows that took 600+ person-hours a year into single-digit hours, backed by nearly 150 test suites. - **An AI-native software-delivery framework.** AI across all seven phases of the software lifecycle: parallel agents in isolated git worktrees moving work through plan, implement, review, test, verify and pull request, with human approval gates and auditable runs, validated by 227 automated tests."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Platforms I've built","text":"- **An AI-powered SDLC and project-management platform.** I architected and delivered it as sole developer, and it replaced a commercial vendor tool for $3M+ in annual savings. Full-stack React/TypeScript + FastAPI + PostgreSQL on Azure, with AI document ingestion. AT&T's CTO called it out on a company-wide broadcast as the kind of execution the company wants in future leaders. - **A national network-analytics platform.** Rebuilt from scratch as sole developer (React, FastAPI, MongoDB); it became the backbone of a data pipeline supporting $52M+ in reported business benefits. - **A self-improving migration platform.** Brought in by the network org to fix how hundreds of legacy tools were being migrated. Earlier AI-assisted attempts were one-off and lost their value between migrations. I built a rulebook-driven system of agents, skills, and MCP servers with automated feedback loops, so each migration makes the next one easier instead of starting from zero. - **Data-platform reliability work.** Led the Databricks Runtime upgrade (10.3 to 15.4 LTS) and validated the Unity Catalog migration across stage and prod, then layered automated data-freshness checks and cluster cost-tagging on top. Troubleshooting time dropped from days to hours; silent failures stopped slipping past us."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Tooling and teaching","text":"Some of the highest-leverage things I do at AT&T aren't single platforms. They're tooling and teaching that change how other engineers ship."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Tooling and teaching","text":"- **50+ reusable engineering and agent skills.** When something works, I package it so other engineers and their agents can reuse it: Terraform, Kubernetes, private networking, identity, observability, CI/CD, container hardening and disaster recovery. The one I'm proudest of gives agents everything a developer can do in the Databricks UI, combining the Databricks CLI, its REST APIs and adapted open-source MCP servers. Earlier skill packages of mine (parallel agents via git worktrees, automated Playwright demos, git-history secret remediation) are used by engineers across AT&T. - **Design partner for AI coding tools.** I was a design partner for an internal AI coding assistant, giving its lead developer structured feedback from daily use of Claude Code, Copilot and Windsurf, and part of a ~25-person external design-partner cohort at Windsurf in the same window. In internal AI-assisted engineering metrics I score about 3.5x the top-performer threshold with top-tier cost efficiency. - **AI-Fueled Coding (AIFC) Lunch & Learn.** Delivered the CDO's AIFC Lunch & Learn, where I laid out the day-to-day workflows that make this stuff actually productive instead of cosmetically impressive. Inside the org I'm the go-to person for it, which mostly means showing up to someone's stuck workflow and pairing through it."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Tooling and teaching","text":"One example was unblocking a colleague's Power BI / Copilot / Power Automate pipeline that had been stuck for weeks; another was bootstrapping a Databricks Unity Catalog access dashboard with reusable Azure CLI skills and an enrichment integration into AT&T's directory. - **Q2 GenAI Hackathon (top three).** Solo-built a network-analytics chatbot using locally-hosted Ollama LLMs. First time I'd built anything around fully-local inference; the experience is most of what got me serious about the gateway/edge story I write about now."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"Cross-org and program work","text":"Some of the things I'm proudest of were the org-level work I picked up alongside the platforms.\n\n- **Multi-Business-Partner ownership.** I was the only ATL TDP supporting multiple Business Partners at once, running parallel product-owner, developer, DevOps, and architect streams across different initiatives. It's most of what taught me how to actually ship inside the org.\n- **Enterprise calendar and event-management tool.** Built ~95% of a Power Automate system used daily by 100+ TDP members in Atlanta. After leadership picked it up, it's being expanded into the national TDP program.\n- **Microsoft Platforms Committee.** Led the committee solo after my co-lead departed, running training and Lunch-and-Learn events on Azure, Power Platform, and Microsoft 365 for the cohort.\n- **Company security Capture the Flag.** First-time CTF participant; I placed 17th of ~160, mostly by leaning hard on AI tooling to compensate for inexperience. The lesson was about applying AI outside its usual lane."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"How the work has landed","text":"A few markers that the work is actually landing: the CTO callout on a company-wide broadcast mentioned above, being cited as a best-practice example for AI-assisted engineering in the org, and the move into my current Senior Data/AI Engineer role in February 2026. The point isn't the recognition; it's that the work is being measured and the measurements line up."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"On my own time","text":"I build a lot of small things to keep my intuitions sharp. The breadth is deliberate; it's how I learn what these tools can and can't actually do. The headline ones, with live no-login demos, are on the [projects page]()."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"On my own time","text":"- **PrintGen.** An AI 3D-print marketplace: text- and image-to-3D generation feeding a local Bambu Lab printer pipeline that slices and prints over the LAN. The fun of it is crossing the software-to-physical-hardware boundary. - **URFMP (Universal Robot Fleet Management Platform).** A fleet-management platform for industrial robots from heterogeneous vendors (UR, ABB, FANUC). TypeScript monorepo, PostgreSQL, Docker. Live demo dashboard at [urfmp.vercel.app](https://urfmp.vercel.app) running a simulated fleet, no login. - **Hybrid Quantum-Classical Starter Kit.** A working implementation of the variational hybrid pattern (a classical optimizer driving short quantum circuits), the same skeleton VQE and QAOA use. Built in Qiskit, D-Wave Ocean, and quimb. Its organizing principle is honesty: every quantum kernel ships next to a competitive classical baseline on the same input, because as of 2026 nearly every quantum-advantage claim on a useful problem has been matched by an improved classical algorithm within a year or two. A repo that shows quantum results in isolation teaches the wrong mental model. - **Financial Analysis Agent System.** A multi-user finance dashboard aggregating Schwab, Coinbase, and Finnhub, with two AI layers: a Claude Code `/invest` skill for portfolio analysis and an in-app Gemini agent with read/write access to user preferences."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"On my own time","text":"Next.js 16, Supabase with row-level security. - **Kalshi Investment Assistant.** An autonomous edge-finding engine running 10 strategies across 40,000+ prediction markets every five minutes, fusing eight data sources over WebSocket fan-out, validated against paper trading. - **Legal Modernization Toolkit + SecureCounsel.** A Claude Code plugin and Python toolkit helping small law firms adopt AI without leaking client data or waiving privilege. Built initially for a real Alpharetta estate-planning firm engagement, with a Presidio-based de-identification core and reversible pseudonymization. The most regulated context I've shipped into outside AT&T. - **SynthSeed.** An AI companion app where users hatch and raise a unique personality from a randomized seed. Starts nonverbal and learns to speak; develops a persistent personality over weeks of interaction. React Native + Supabase + Anthropic Claude + Mem0/Zep Graphiti for long-horizon memory. - **A small cluster of AI-tooling experiments.** MCPBuilder (a builder for MCP servers), agentic-workflows (framework for agentic workflows), Invest4Me (an MCP-driven portfolio supervisor). These are the projects that taught me what MCP actually is and what an agentic workflow looks like once you've shipped one rather than read about it."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"On my own time","text":"- **A resell bot** I built for selling my and my family's things on eBay and Facebook Marketplace when the official APIs weren't an option. Small, practical, and a tidy demonstration of the [context-injection ideas](/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html) I write about. It stays private because it touches buyer PII and my own financial scans, but it gets used.\n\nThere are 20-some of these in total. Quantum cybersecurity, financial systems, AI tooling, networking, 3D printing and IoT, prediction markets, legal-tech consulting; same person, different domains. I pick up whatever the problem requires."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"A bit of background","text":"I started at AT&T as a summer intern in 2021 and stayed across three summers and a four-year Technology Development Program track. The middle internship was on a quantum-cybersecurity team (one of four interns) supporting the government-mandated migration of 24,000+ applications to quantum-safe encryption, presenting to VPs and the CISO on Grover's Algorithm and cryptographic agility. That's where the quantum interest started.\n\nIn February 2026 I transitioned out of TDP into a Senior Data/AI Engineer role in the Chief Data Office, which is where I am now. Most of the AT&T work above sits inside that arc.\n\nI studied at the University of Georgia's Terry College of Business: an M.S. in Business Analytics (Magna Cum Laude) and a B.B.A. in Finance (Cum Laude, Zell Miller Scholarship). The finance background is why so many of my side projects end up pointed at markets. I also hold the Microsoft Azure Data Fundamentals (DP-900) and Azure AI Fundamentals (AI-900) certifications."},{"source":"About page","url":"/","heading":"What I'm looking for next","text":"A team of fast-moving builders I can both contribute to and learn from, where working at the bleeding edge of AI is the default, and where an enterprise-scale, battle-tested perspective is treated as an asset rather than baggage. I'm most interested in frontier AI labs and the teams building the tools the rest of us code with.\n\nIf that sounds like your team, I'd love to talk."},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"","text":"Engineer who has shipped AI and non-AI platforms end to end in a 200,000-person regulated enterprise as architect, developer, DevOps engineer and product owner, and who is ahead of most in using AI coding agents, MCP and agent orchestration to build whatever the work needs.","lead":true},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"Experience","text":"### AT&T, Senior Data/AI Engineer, Chief Data Office\n*Atlanta, GA · Feb 2026 – Present*\n\n- **Technical lead and architect** of a confidential multi-tenant orchestration platform on Kubernetes; guided a ~10-person team, most more senior, from governance approval through Azure build-out to a working environment in about four months, with 166 test files covering load, penetration and chaos testing. - As **primary developer**, took a real-time geofence event platform from first code to production in about five months, then shipped a second release with ~10x the capabilities, cutting **600+ person-hours a year to single digits**. - Architected an **AI-native delivery framework** spanning all seven SDLC phases: parallel agents in isolated git worktrees take work from plan to pull request behind human approval gates, validated by 227 automated tests. - Designed a **self-improving migration platform** for hundreds of legacy network tools, where a central rulebook drives agents, skills and MCP servers and feedback loops make each migration easier than the last. - Packaged **50+ reusable engineering and agent skills** used by engineers across AT&T, including one that gives agents everything a developer can do in the Databricks UI through its CLI, REST APIs and MCP servers."},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"Experience","text":"- Score about **3.5x the top-performer threshold** in internal AI-assisted engineering metrics, cited as the org's best-practice example; teach AI-fueled coding across the Chief Data Office and serve as a design partner for an internal AI coding assistant and Windsurf's ~25-person cohort.\n\n### AT&T, Technology Development Program, Chief Data Office\n*Atlanta, GA · 2024 – 2026*\n\n- As sole developer, built an **AI-powered SDLC and project-management platform** that replaced a vendor tool for **$3M+ in annual savings**; called out by AT&T's CTO on a company-wide broadcast.\n- Rebuilt a **national network-analytics platform** as sole developer, the backbone of a data pipeline supporting **$52M+ in reported business benefits**.\n- Led a team building a Power Apps and Power Automate **calendar system** on SharePoint and Teams, used daily by **100+ TDP members** and expanding nationally; built about 95% of it and trained others to maintain it.\n- Built a **RAG tool that analyzes software license contracts**; placed **top 3 in a GenAI hackathon** with a solo-built, locally hosted LLM for network-config analysis; led the Databricks Runtime and Unity Catalog migration.\n\n### AT&T, Rotational Internship Program\n*Atlanta, GA · 2021 – 2023*\n\n- On a 4-person quantum-cybersecurity team, supported moving **24,000+ applications to quantum-safe encryption**; presented to VPs and the CISO."},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"Selected Projects","text":"- **Kalshi Investment Assistant:** 10 strategies scanning 40,000+ prediction markets every five minutes, paper-traded.\n- **Financial Analysis Agent System:** Schwab, Coinbase and Finnhub dashboard with Claude and Gemini agents."},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"Education","text":"**University of Georgia, Terry College of Business.** M.S. Business Analytics, 2024 (3.85, Magna Cum Laude); B.B.A. Finance, 2023 (3.73, Cum Laude)."},{"source":"Resume","url":"/resume/","heading":"Skills","text":"**Languages and frameworks:** Python, TypeScript, SQL, React, Next.js, FastAPI, Node.js\n\n**AI:** Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Devin, Codex, Windsurf, MCP servers, agent orchestration, Azure OpenAI, Ollama\n\n**Platform:** Azure, Kubernetes, Helm, Terraform, Entra ID, GitHub Actions, Grafana, Databricks, PostgreSQL, MongoDB\n\n**Certifications:** Microsoft Azure DP-900, AI-900"},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"","text":"Colleagues come to me a lot. The conversation almost always goes the same way.\n\n\"My agent isn't doing what I want.\"\n\n\"What do you want it to do?\"\n\n\"I want it to do X. But the problem is, when it does X, it needs to also handle Y, because Z is going to come up later and if it doesn't account for that, the whole thing breaks.\"\n\n\"Did you tell it that?\"\n\nA pause. Then: *\"...no.\"*\n\nThat's the easy case. The fix is literally what they just said. Tell the agent the same paragraph you told me: every constraint, every edge case, every \"by the way.\" The agent isn't psychic. If you can explain the problem to a person, you can explain it to the agent, and that single act is the difference between a useless output and a finished one. Five minutes of effort, no exotic prompting technique required.\n\nI used to think that was the whole story. It isn't, and the part that isn't is what I want to write about.","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The case that's actually hard","text":"Sometimes a colleague comes to me, I ask what they want the agent to do, and they can't tell me.\n\nNot because they're hiding anything. Not because they're being lazy. Because they genuinely don't know.\n\nThey know they need *something*. They know the current situation isn't working. They know there should be a way to use AI to make it better. But when you ask what *better* looks like (what the actual output should be, what success looks like, what they'd do with the result once they had it), the answers come back vague, contradictory, sometimes circular. They want a dashboard, but they don't know what should be on it. They want to automate a workflow, but they can't describe the workflow. They want \"AI to help\" but can't say with what.\n\nThis is not a prompting problem. There is no prompt the person can write, because there is no clear thought in their head yet to convert into text. The agent isn't failing them. *Their inability to define the goal is failing them*, and the agent is just exposing it."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The case that's actually hard","text":"The first few times this happened to me, I treated it like the easy case. I tried to coax the prompt out. *Okay, what are you trying to do? What does the data look like? What should the output be?* The answers stayed vague. Eventually I learned that what these colleagues needed wasn't prompting advice. They needed the kind of help a consultant gives someone whose business is on fire and they don't know why. They needed someone to sit down, ask questions, redraw the problem until it had clear edges, and only *then*, once the goal was sharp, write the prompt.\n\nMost of the time the prompt is the easy part. The hard part is the half-hour conversation before the prompt, where you figure out what the actual goal is."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"Why this is happening more, not less","text":"You might expect this problem to fade as AI tools improve. The opposite is happening, and I think it's because the bar for \"what you can plausibly attempt with AI\" has shot up faster than most people's ability to keep up.\n\nFive years ago, if you couldn't articulate a clear goal, you wouldn't even start. The technical barrier filtered out the unclear ideas before they hit code. You had to learn enough about, say, building a dashboard to know what you wanted on it before anyone could help you. The learning forced the clarity.\n\nNow anyone can open a chat window and say \"build me a dashboard.\" The agent will happily try. And if you don't know what you want, you'll get a dashboard you don't want, decide AI doesn't work, and walk away.\n\nThe technical filter is gone. The clarity filter is not. AI has made the gap between \"people who can define their goals clearly\" and \"people who can't\" the most important productivity divide in technical work, and the divide is widening."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"How you actually get out of failure mode #2","text":"If you find yourself in this position, knowing you need *something* but not being able to say what, here's the only path out I know of, because it's the one I had to walk myself.\n\nBuild the mental model by playing.\n\nWhen I started, I was the colleague in the second case. I knew I should be using AI more aggressively. I knew there was a productivity multiplier I wasn't getting. But I couldn't have told you specifically what I wanted the AI to do, because I didn't yet have a feel for *what AI could do*. The vocabulary wasn't there. The intuitions weren't there. I'd ask vague questions, get vague results, and conclude the tool was overhyped.\n\nWhat changed it was playing. Not on anything important. On small projects with no stakes. A script to clean up a folder of files I'd been meaning to organize. A weekend experiment with a new framework I'd been curious about. A toy version of a thing I half-remembered wanting to build. Most of these failed in some way: produced something weird, missed the point, took more iteration than I'd budgeted for. That was fine. The point wasn't the output; the point was that each one taught me a little more about what AI could actually do, and just as importantly, what it couldn't."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"How you actually get out of failure mode #2","text":"After enough of these, something shifts. You stop asking \"can AI do X\" and start automatically knowing which kind of X is well-suited to AI and which isn't. You stop being surprised when the agent nails something hard or stumbles on something easy, because you've seen the pattern before. The vagueness in your goals starts to resolve, because you finally have a model of the territory you're trying to define a goal *in*.\n\nAnd then your own judgment kicks in. You stop deferring to whoever sounds confident on Twitter about which tool to use. You stop being paralyzed by every new release. You can evaluate a new agent or a new feature in five minutes because you've already calibrated what good looks like by feel.\n\nThis is the part that's hard to convey to people in failure mode #2: the only way out is to fail on enough small things that you build the intuition. You can't read your way to it. You can't course your way to it. You have to put in the reps where the stakes are low enough that failure is fine, and you have to do it on enough different kinds of problems that the pattern starts to generalize."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"How you actually get out of failure mode #2","text":"People who don't do this stay stuck. They keep coming back asking \"but how do I get the agent to...\" about the same kinds of tasks, and the answer is always the same: you need more reps. Not on this. On other things. So that when you come back to this, you've got something to define the goal from."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The temperament question","text":"This is the part I'm cautious writing because it sounds like I'm dunking on people, and I'm not. But there's a real pattern worth naming.\n\nNot everyone is going to thrive with these tools, and it's not about intelligence. It's about a specific temperament. The temperament that says: *I want to be able to articulate what I want; if I can't, that's a problem to solve, not a thing to work around.* The temperament that's willing to slow down at the front of a task to gain speed in the middle. The temperament that takes \"tell me exactly what you want\" as an invitation to think more clearly, not a frustration to vent about.\n\nPeople with that temperament get extraordinary leverage from AI, the kind that feels almost unfair when you watch it from outside. People without it keep complaining that the tools don't work, and the tools, technically, will work fine."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The temperament question","text":"This isn't fixed. It's trainable. But the want has to come from somewhere: curiosity, ambition, frustration with your own sloppiness, whatever lights you up. If the want isn't there, no amount of better prompts and better tools will close the gap. The people who keep iterating, who try the suggestion and come back with a sharper question, are the ones who will be running the next set of projects whether anyone hands it to them or not. The ones who come once, get the answer, and never refine the muscle; they get the immediate win and then plateau.\n\nI'm not saying everyone has to be the first kind of person. Plenty of people do excellent work without ever wanting to live at the front of the AI curve. But there's no longer a quiet middle ground in technical work where you can stay current without engaging. The middle is collapsing. You're either training the muscle or you're falling behind, and the falling-behind is going to get faster from here, not slower."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The thing I really want you to take away","text":"The bottleneck has moved from your ability to execute to your ability to define what you want.\n\nFor most of software engineering's history, execution was the hard part. You needed the languages, the tools, the syntax, the frameworks, the systems. The defining part was assumed; if you could code it, you must have already figured out what *it* was.\n\nAI inverts this. Execution is largely solved, or solvable, with enough back-and-forth with a competent agent. Defining-what-you-want is now the gating skill, the place leverage compounds, and the place most people are quietly under-investing.\n\nThat skill is the most valuable thing you can build right now. More valuable than knowing which model to use, which tool to pick, which framework is hot this month. Those things change every quarter. The ability to look at a situation, define the goal sharply, and communicate it without ambiguity: that's permanent, and it's the thing AI rewards more than any specific technical skill."},{"source":"Post \"The bottleneck has moved\" (May 24, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/24/the-bottleneck-has-moved.html","heading":"The thing I really want you to take away","text":"So: the next time your agent gives you something you didn't want, before you blame the model, run the exercise. Write down what you'd tell a colleague who walked up to your desk and asked. If you can write that paragraph and the agent still gets it wrong, the problem is the agent. If you can't write that paragraph, the problem was never the agent. It's upstream, and the only way to fix it is to think harder before you type. Or, if the thinking won't come, to go play on something small until it does.\n\nThat's the work. It's not glamorous. It's not what the demos sell. But it's the thing that separates the people getting wild leverage from these tools and the people who keep wondering why everyone else seems to be playing a different game.\n\n---\n\n*David Morgan is a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office, where he builds production AI systems and orchestration platforms. More writing at [djmorgan26.github.io](https://djmorgan26.github.io); code at [github.com/djmorgan26](https://github.com/djmorgan26).*"},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"","text":"I switched between three AI coding tools last year, and somewhere in the middle of it I stopped having to relearn anything.\n\nThe first time I moved from GitHub Copilot to Claude Code, I expected a learning curve. There wasn't one, or not the one I expected. The buttons were different, the file names were different, the docs used different words, but the thing I was actually doing was identical. I was taking knowledge that lived in my head and putting it somewhere the agent would read it at the right moment. Copilot called the artifact a prompt file, Windsurf called it a workflow, Claude calls it a command. Each tool also has a second form (context the agent pulls in on its own instead of waiting for you to invoke it) which Claude calls a skill, and AT&T's internal tooling has its own name for it. Underneath, they are the same primitive, and once I saw that, switching tools became a translation exercise instead of a learning one.\n\nThat primitive is what this post is about. I've started calling the discipline of using it well **Strategical Context Injection**, and I think it's the single highest-leverage idea I can hand to someone who already knows how to talk to an agent and wants to know what to get good at next.","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"They're all the same primitive","text":"Skills, workflows, prompts, MCP servers, `agent.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, memories. They look like a zoo of different features. They are not. Every one of them is a bundle of context you inject at a particular moment so the agent doesn't have to rediscover what you already know.\n\nA command, or Copilot's prompt file, is a context bundle *you* trigger by name. A workflow is the same idea with an ordered set of steps baked in: still something you invoke, just multi-step. A skill is the one that trips people up: a context bundle the *agent* reaches for on its own when it judges a task needs it, without you invoking anything. That auto-retrieval, the agent deciding what to load and when, is the primitive Anthropic introduced and the other tools are now copying. An MCP server is a context bundle that also knows how to *fetch* live context and take actions. A `CLAUDE.md` is a context bundle that loads every session. A memory is a context bundle the agent wrote for itself. The surface differs (when it loads, who triggers it, whether it can call out to the world), but the job is the same: put the right knowledge in front of the model at the right time."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"They're all the same primitive","text":"This is why the tool-switching cost is so low once you internalize it. You're not learning Windsurf or Claude or Copilot. You're learning *what context this task needs and when it needs it*, and then expressing that in whatever syntax the current tool happens to use. The syntax is the easy part. We used to spend real effort learning the differences between programming languages; AI made that mostly irrelevant. Then we spent effort learning the differences between agent harnesses; that's going the same way. What doesn't go away is the judgment about which context matters."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Naming the discipline","text":"Strategical Context Injection is the practice of deciding *which* context to inject, *when* to inject it, and *how* to package it so it travels.\n\nI'm naming it because the unnamed version of this skill is invisible, and invisible skills don't get taught or transferred. People watch someone get great results from an agent and conclude they're a better prompter, or they picked the right model, or they got lucky. Usually what actually happened is that the person had already done the context work. The relevant standards, the examples, the constraints were sitting right where the agent would read them, so the \"prompt\" could be three lines. The leverage wasn't in the prompt. It was in the context that was already in place before the prompt was typed.\n\nIf you take one thing from this: stop optimizing prompts and start optimizing context. The prompt is the last five percent."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"When to inject, and when to let the agent reach for it","text":"Not all context should be loaded all the time. The art is matching the delivery mechanism to how often the context is needed and how big it is.\n\nIf something is true for every task in a project (the stack, the conventions, where things live, what \"done\" means), it goes in the always-loaded layer (`CLAUDE.md` or its equivalent). If something is only relevant to a specific kind of task, it should be a skill the agent reaches for when that task shows up, so you're not paying the context cost on every unrelated request. If something is large, changes often, or lives in an external system, don't paste it in at all; give the agent a tool or an MCP server that fetches it fresh when needed.\n\nGetting this wrong is expensive in both directions. Inject too little and the agent rediscovers the same facts every session, or worse, guesses. Inject too much and you bloat the context, slow everything down, and bury the signal that actually mattered. Claude's recent move toward letting agents manage their own working memory (deciding what to keep, what to summarize, what to drop) is a direct response to this exact problem, and it's a good example of a genuinely new idea rather than a reskinned one. Most \"new features\" are the same primitive with a fresh label; a few solve a real problem the primitive created. Learning to tell those apart is most of what staying current actually is."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Make it portable, or it dies with you","text":"A context bundle that only works on your machine, in your head, with your undocumented assumptions, is a personal productivity trick. A context bundle someone else can pick up and use is infrastructure. The gap between those two is almost entirely effort you have to choose to spend, and most people don't.\n\nSo spend it. Concretely:\n\n**Assume nobody will try.** Most people will not put in the effort to adopt your skill, even a good one, if it asks anything of them up front. Make the cost of trying as close to zero as you can: a clear name, a one-line description of when to use it, a working example they can run without reading anything else. The onboarding *is* the product. An agent that can walk a new person through the handoff beats a perfect skill nobody opens.\n\n**Parameterize instead of hardcoding.** The version that has your username and your file paths baked in helps exactly one person. Pull those out into inputs and the same bundle serves the whole team."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Make it portable, or it dies with you","text":"**Reference; don't snapshot.** This is the one that bites everyone. Do not write a document that compares Windsurf and VS Code feature by feature. That data is wrong within weeks, and now you've got an authoritative-looking file actively lying to the agent. Link to the official docs that get updated when the product changes, and let the agent read the current version. The same rule applies to anything that moves: point at the source of truth instead of copying its state into your context. If you find yourself reading agent output that confidently asserts relationships in your project that *used to be true*, that's the tell: somewhere you snapshotted something you should have referenced.\n\nKeep the bundles themselves pristine. Timestamps, changelogs, version markers, consistent naming conventions. Boring metadata is what keeps a context bundle honest a year after you wrote it."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Once it's shared, it's a dependency","text":"The part nobody has fully figured out yet is what happens after a bundle stops being yours. The moment other people and other agents depend on it, it isn't a note anymore. It's a dependency, and it earns the same discipline you'd give any other one. This is newer territory and the conventions are still forming, but the shape of it is already clear, and it's borrowed straight from how we ship code.\n\nVersion them, and pin to a version. A change to a skill is a change to behavior, often *invisible* behavior, and \"I tweaked the skill\" is a miserable answer to \"why did every agent on the team start doing this yesterday?\" Give each bundle a version and a changelog so a change is something you can see, attribute, and roll back. Then let the things that depend on it pin to a known version and upgrade deliberately, the same way you pin a library, so an improvement to a shared bundle never lands as a silent regression in someone else's workflow."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Once it's shared, it's a dependency","text":"Catching that regression in the first place is the harder half, and it's the part I'm actively wrestling with right now: evaluating a context bundle the way you'd eval any other piece of an agentic system. It's tractable when you own the harness, since you control the logs, the metrics, and the traces, so you can measure a skill's effect directly. On someone else's harness (Copilot, Claude Code, whatever your company hands you), those signals are exactly the ones you can't reach, so you end up stitching together your own: hooks, ad-hoc data collection, whatever the tool lets you instrument. I've been building one such strategy, and I'll be honest that it's one strategy among many. The industry hasn't settled on a standard for evaluating agentic systems yet, and I'd bet most teams doing it seriously have quietly rolled their own."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Once it's shared, it's a dependency","text":"This is where documentation standards stop being a nicety and start being load-bearing. At one bundle, a clear description and a working example are a courtesy. At fifty, across a team, they're the only thing separating a library from a junk drawer: a name, a one-line \"use this when,\" declared inputs, an example, a version, a last-updated date. That metadata is what lets a person, or an agent running the handoff, trust a bundle without reading its internals. The discipline that felt like overhead on your first skill is exactly what makes the hundredth one usable."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Standardization is the unsexy multiplier","text":"The biggest gains I've seen didn't come from one brilliant skill. They came from a *system* of mediocre-but-consistent ones.\n\nOn a migration project at work, the problem wasn't that people couldn't use AI. It was that each engineer's AI-assisted work was a one-off. Nothing compounded. The fix wasn't a better prompt; it was a centralized rulebook every agent read from, so that an answer captured once made every future migration easier than the last. Standardize the conventions, standardize where context lives, standardize how bundles are named and triggered, and the whole team's work starts accumulating instead of evaporating. It's not glamorous. It's the thing that turns individual leverage into organizational leverage.\n\nThis is also where giving back matters. When you solve something, capture it in a bundle and make it portable enough to hand off. We can all do this. Most people just stop one step short; they get the win and never package it. The step from \"it works for me\" to \"anyone can use this\" is small, and it's where most of the durable value is."},{"source":"Post \"Strategical Context Injection: the unifying frame for skills, workflows, prompts, and .md files\" (May 25, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/25/strategical-context-injection.html","heading":"Why most people stop at one good skill","text":"Here's the pattern I keep seeing. Someone writes one skill, it works, they're delighted, and then they stop. They've proven the concept to themselves and they treat that as the finish line.\n\nThe finish line is the system, not the first skill. The first skill teaches you the primitive. The leverage shows up when you start seeing every repeated explanation, every \"let me tell you how we do this here,\" every paragraph you've now typed to an agent twice, as a context bundle waiting to be written down once. That shift, from prompting reactively to injecting context deliberately, is the whole game. The tools will keep renaming the primitive. The discipline of knowing which context to inject, when, and how to make it travel doesn't change, and it's the thing worth getting good at.\n\n---\n\n*David Morgan is a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office, where he builds production AI systems and orchestration platforms. More writing at [djmorgan26.github.io](https://djmorgan26.github.io); code at [github.com/djmorgan26](https://github.com/djmorgan26).*"},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"","text":"We are building AI systems faster than we can measure what they're worth, and burning compute faster than we can produce it. Those two gaps are about to collide, and when they do the first casualty will be a question nobody on my team can currently answer: who gets the tokens?\n\nI don't mean that philosophically. I mean it as a budgeting problem that lands on a real person's desk every quarter. Someone has to decide how much an engineer, a team, or a job function is allowed to spend on model calls. Right now that decision is being made (when it's made at all) with no instrument that connects spend to value. We have invoices. We have token counts. We have nothing that says this thousand-dollar week of inference produced more than that hundred-dollar week did. The number we'd actually need to make the call is the one number we don't have.","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The metric doesn't exist yet","text":"The thing we can't measure is the ratio of productivity to tokens, and almost every budgeting decision quietly depends on it.\n\nThink about what we *can* see. I can pull exactly how many tokens a developer consumed last month, down to the model and the hour. What I cannot tell you is whether those tokens shipped a feature, chased a hallucination in a circle for three days, or quietly drafted the doc that unblocked four other people. The token count is precise and the value is invisible, which is the worst possible combination. It tempts you to manage the number you can see instead of the outcome you can't. I've watched smart organizations do exactly that with story points and lines of code for twenty years. Tokens are the same trap with a bigger bill attached.\n\nAnd measuring software productivity was already hard *before* AI got in the loop. The classic proxies (commits, PRs, tickets closed) got noisier the moment an agent could generate a hundred lines as easily as one. The output went up and the signal in the output went down. So we're not refining an existing metric. We're trying to invent one from scratch, on a moving target, while the spend that depends on it grows every month."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"Until the metric exists, every budget is a guess","text":"Here's the uncomfortable consequence. If you can't measure productivity-per-token, then any token budget you set is essentially random.\n\nNot random in the sense of careless; the people setting them are careful. Random in the sense that there is no defensible basis for the number. Why does this team get 10 million tokens a month and that one get 5? Because somebody felt it was about right. Because last quarter's bill was roughly that. Because the loud engineer asked. Strip away the rationalizations and you find a guess wearing a spreadsheet. As I keep putting it: setting a hard token budget today is almost random guessing about whose hands to put money into. We don't have the data that would make it anything else.\n\nWhich is why the cap, when there is one, is so soft. Limits get set generously and enforced reluctantly, because everyone involved half-knows the number is arbitrary and nobody wants to be the person who throttled the engineer who was about to ship something real. So in practice the policy is: very, very soft limits, even between people doing the same job. Nobody knows how to dynamically budget tokens well, so mostly nobody does. They set a high ceiling, watch the invoice, and hope."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"Soft limits are the right interim policy anyway","text":"I want to defend the soft limit, because it sounds like a cop-out and it isn't.\n\nWhen you're missing the one measurement that would justify a hard rule, a hard rule is the *more* dangerous choice, not the safer one. A tight cap optimizes a number you've already admitted is disconnected from value, and the failure mode is brutal: you starve the exact use that was paying for itself while leaving the wasteful one untouched, because you couldn't tell them apart. A soft limit costs you some money on waste. A hard limit set blind costs you the upside, and the upside is the entire reason you're spending the money. Until I can show that a given thousand dollars of inference returned less than another thousand did, I'd rather overspend on a real engineer doing real work than enforce a clean rule that's quietly throttling my best leverage. The soft limit isn't the answer. It's the honest placeholder you keep until you've built the thing that lets you do better."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The answer probably lives at the gateway","text":"Here's where I think the real answer comes from, and it's not from any single model vendor. It's from the boring layer in the middle that everyone routes through.\n\nThe model gateway (the thing that sits between your code and every model API, so you can swap providers without rewriting anything) is the one place that sees all of it. Every request, every model choice, every token, across every team, in one funnel. That's exactly the vantage point a budget needs. So the eventual answer is probably this: the gateway holds the budget, routes each request to whichever model can satisfy it cheapest while still clearing the quality bar, and reports productivity-per-token back to teams in a form they can actually act on. Not a raw invoice. An answer to \"are we getting our money's worth, and where aren't we?\""},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The answer probably lives at the gateway","text":"LiteLLM is the most obvious candidate to grow into that role, because it already owns the gateway position; it's where a lot of teams already centralize their routing, and the budget is the natural next thing to hang off a layer that already sees everything. Ollama is the piece that handles the other half: a real local and edge fallback, so the requests that don't need a frontier model never pay frontier prices, and some never leave the building. OpenRouter covers breadth, one interface onto a wide spread of models so \"cheapest that clears the bar\" has a real menu to choose from rather than two options. None of this is exotic. The components are shipping today. What's missing is the wiring: nobody has connected routing, budgeting, and a credible productivity signal into one loop that closes. We're not there yet. We're not even close. But the pieces exist, and that's usually the part that takes longest."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The answer probably lives at the gateway","text":"I'll be honest about the hard part, which is the same hard part as everywhere else in this post. Routing by *cost* is solved; the gateway knows the price of every token. Routing by *quality* is not, because \"did this response meet the bar\" is the productivity measurement again, wearing a different hat. So the gateway is where the answer lives, but it can't fully deliver it until we've cracked the measurement problem upstream. The infrastructure is ahead of the metric. That's the whole story of this moment, compressed into one component."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The compute under all of this is borrowed time","text":"Step back from the accounting problem and there's a physical one underneath it, on a longer clock but a harder deadline.\n\nWe are consuming compute faster than we can build it. The reason token budgets feel tight isn't only that we can't measure value. It's that the underlying supply is genuinely constrained, and demand is climbing a curve that the buildout of data centers and power and chips can't match in real time. Right now the hyperscalers (Google, AWS, Microsoft) hold the edge here, because raw infrastructure at this scale is the one thing you can't conjure with a clever idea. But I read that advantage as a phase, not a permanent state. It's an edge that lasts exactly as long as physical capacity is the binding constraint, and physical constraints are the kind engineers eventually engineer around."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"The compute under all of this is borrowed time","text":"The unlock people point to is putting data centers in space: free cooling, uninterrupted solar, none of the terrestrial fights over land and grid capacity. I think that genuinely rebalances things when it matures. The problem is *when*. Timing matters more than the eventual outcome here, because the token economy could destabilize before the physical engineering catches up. If demand keeps outrunning supply, and the metrics to allocate scarce supply rationally still don't exist, and the next generation of capacity is still years out, that's three unsolved problems converging, and if it gets much worse before we solve the hard engineering, things get awkward fast. That's not a prediction of collapse. It's a statement about which problems need to be solved in which order, and a worry that the order isn't lining up in our favor."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"What I'd actually do about it","text":"I don't get to fix the compute supply or invent the productivity metric this quarter, so the honest question is what to do in the meantime.\n\nKeep the limits soft, but stop pretending the gateway is just plumbing. Route everything through one, so that the day a credible productivity signal exists you already have the chokepoint to apply it; you've been collecting the data the whole time instead of starting from zero. Push the cheap, local-capable work down to local models now, because that's a real cost lever that doesn't depend on solving measurement first. And treat \"how do we measure whether this spend was worth it\" as a live engineering problem on the roadmap, not a finance question to be deferred, because it's the input every other decision here is waiting on.\n\nThe deeper point is that this is the same skill that matters everywhere else in AI work right now: being able to reason clearly about what outcome you actually want, while staying open-minded enough to take the answer from a machine, or from the person who built the gateway, instead of from your own priors. We figured out how to build with these systems years before we figured out how to account for them. The building is the fun part and we got good at it fast. The accounting is the unglamorous part that decides whether any of it is sustainable, and it's the part still wide open. I'd rather work on the open problem."},{"source":"Post \"We have no way to measure AI productivity\" (May 28, 2026)","url":"/2026/05/28/measuring-ai-productivity.html","heading":"What I'd actually do about it","text":"---\n\n*David Morgan is a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office, where he builds production AI systems and orchestration platforms. More writing at [djmorgan26.github.io](https://djmorgan26.github.io); code at [github.com/djmorgan26](https://github.com/djmorgan26).*"},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"","text":"Five years ago, if you'd told a room full of serious people that AI would be writing production code, winning Nobel-adjacent science prizes, and forcing companies to rewrite their capital plans by 2026, most of them would have smiled politely. The timeline was supposed to be longer. It wasn't. AI got here sooner than almost everyone thought, and the people who were early didn't have better information — they just took the curve seriously instead of rounding it down to zero.\n\nI think quantum computing is sitting in that exact spot right now, and the people who dismissed AI's timeline are about to make the same mistake twice. But there's a second-order point that matters even more than the timing, and it's the one I actually want to make in this post: **AI and quantum have started accelerating each other.** Not metaphorically. In specific, published, reproducible ways. AI is helping us solve quantum's hardest problem, quantum is starting to do physics that will feed back into AI, and the discovery cycle that connects them is getting shorter every year. That's a flywheel. And flywheels don't move linearly — they spin up.\n\nLet me show you the evidence, then where I think it goes, then what it means for money and for the companies you're watching.","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The thing people miss: it's a loop, not two races","text":"The usual framing is that AI and quantum are two separate frontier bets, each with its own timeline, competing for the same headlines and the same capital. That framing is wrong, and it's wrong in a way that causes you to underestimate both."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The thing people miss: it's a loop, not two races","text":"Start with the direction everyone forgets: **AI is fixing quantum's single biggest obstacle.** Quantum computers store information in *qubits* — quantum bits, the equivalent of the 1s and 0s in an ordinary computer, except a qubit can hold a blend of both at once, which is where the power comes from. The problem is they're fragile: qubits *decohere* (lose their delicate quantum state to heat, vibration, or a stray magnetic field — essentially forgetting what they were holding), errors creep in, and for decades the wall between \"interesting demo\" and \"useful machine\" has been *error correction* (spreading one trustworthy \"logical\" qubit across many physical ones so the machine can catch and fix its own mistakes). In November 2024, Google DeepMind published [AlphaQubit](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-deepmind/alphaqubit-quantum-error-correction/) in *Nature*: a neural-network *decoder* (the component that reads the messy error signals and works out what actually went wrong), built on the same Transformer architecture that powers large language models, that identifies quantum errors more accurately than the hand-engineered methods the field had relied on."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The thing people miss: it's a loop, not two races","text":"It made 6% fewer errors than tensor-network decoders (accurate but far too slow to be practical) and 30% fewer than correlated matching (the fast, scalable approach), and it held up when tested on systems of up to 241 qubits. Read that again: the architecture we invented to predict the next word in a sentence is now one of the best tools we have for keeping a quantum computer coherent. AI walked over to quantum's biggest roadblock and started moving it."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The thing people miss: it's a loop, not two races","text":"Now the other direction. In October 2025, Google ran an algorithm called [Quantum Echoes](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/research/quantum-echoes-willow-verifiable-quantum-advantage/) on its 105-qubit Willow chip and clocked it at **13,000× faster** than the best classical algorithm running on one of the world's fastest supercomputers — and, crucially, it was the first *verifiable* quantum advantage. \"Quantum advantage\" just means a quantum machine doing something faster than any normal computer realistically can; the new and important word is *verifiable* — the result could be independently reproduced on other quantum hardware rather than just asserted. That's what makes it different from Google's contested 2019 \"quantum supremacy\" claim, which IBM disputed precisely because no one could check it. And the application isn't a toy: the technique measures how disturbances ripple through quantum systems, which maps directly onto molecular structure, NMR (nuclear magnetic resonance — the same physics behind MRI scans, and a chemist's main tool for figuring out a molecule's shape), and the simulation of materials and drugs. Those simulations are exactly the kind of expensive, data-starved problems that bottleneck AI today. Quantum doesn't just compute faster — it can generate the high-fidelity physical data that the next generation of scientific AI needs to train on."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The thing people miss: it's a loop, not two races","text":"So: AI makes quantum work, quantum generates the data and simulation muscle that makes AI smarter about the physical world, and around it goes. NVIDIA has already shipped the connective tissue — its NVQLink interconnect is built precisely to wire quantum processors into GPU-based AI systems. The plumbing for the loop is being laid right now."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"AI already compressed the discovery cycle — that's the leading indicator","text":"If you want to know how fast the quantum half of this loop will move, look at what AI did to its first scientific target, because that's the rehearsal.\n\nIn late 2023, DeepMind's [GNoME](https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/millions-of-new-materials-discovered-with-deep-learning/) predicted **2.2 million new crystal structures** — roughly 380,000 of them *thermodynamically stable* (stable enough to actually exist and hold together in the real world, not just on paper) — in a stretch of work the team estimated would have taken about 800 years of conventional research. Among them: 52,000 new graphene-like compounds (we previously knew of around 1,000) and 528 candidate lithium-ion conductors, the stuff better batteries are made of. And this wasn't a paper that died in simulation. Labs around the world have independently synthesized hundreds of the predicted materials, and an autonomous robotic lab at Berkeley used GNoME's data to make 41 brand-new materials from scratch with almost no human hands involved. Microsoft followed with its own generative model, MatterGen, that designs materials to hit a target property on demand."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"AI already compressed the discovery cycle — that's the leading indicator","text":"Then 2024 happened, and the establishment made it official: **both** the Nobel Prize in Physics (Hopfield and Hinton, for the neural networks underneath all of this) and the Nobel Prize in Chemistry (Hassabis, Jumper, and Baker, for AlphaFold and protein design) went to AI. Two of the oldest, most conservative prizes in science, in the same year, for the same underlying technology. Hassabis called AlphaFold \"the first proof point of AI's incredible potential to accelerate scientific discovery.\" The word that matters there is *accelerate*. The cadence of fundamental discovery — materials, proteins, and soon the quantum-chemistry problems Willow is reaching for (simulating how molecules and electrons actually behave, which classical computers choke on past a certain size) — is no longer set by the speed of human grad-student-years. It's set by compute, and compute is the one thing that compounds.\n\nThat compression is the leading indicator for quantum. The same recipe — AI proposes, automated/quantum systems verify, the verified result trains the next model — is being pointed at the quantum stack itself."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Why the U.S. Treasury just stopped pretending this is private-sector business","text":"Here's the signal that convinced me the institutional money already sees the curve: the government just bought in. Literally bought equity.\n\nOn May 21, 2026, the Department of Commerce announced [letters of intent worth $2.013 billion](https://thequantuminsider.com/2026/05/21/u-s-department-of-commerce-announces-letters-of-intent-with-9-companies-for-2-billion/) to nine quantum computing companies, funded under the CHIPS and Science Act — and as a *condition of every award*, the government takes a minority, non-controlling equity stake in the company. This is not a research grant. It's the United States buying a stake in the quantum industry the way you'd buy into a sector you believe is about to inflect. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick framed it plainly: the administration is \"leading the world into a new era of American innovation.\" The subtext is less polite, and it's all over the coverage — this is about beating China to \"Q-Day,\" the moment a quantum computer can break the encryption protecting banks, military comms, and crypto. Whoever gets there first writes the rules.\n\nThe allocation tells you who Washington thinks the winners are:"},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Why the U.S. Treasury just stopped pretending this is private-sector business","text":"- **IBM — $1 billion**, the largest of the nine, to anchor a new superconducting quantum foundry (a *foundry* is a chip factory; *superconducting* refers to the supercooled circuits IBM uses to make its qubits). IBM is matching it with $1 billion of its own to spin up **Anderon**, a standalone 300mm quantum wafer fab in Albany, New York — a plant that builds quantum chips on the same standard 300-millimeter silicon wafers the ordinary chip industry uses — effectively America's first dedicated quantum chip foundry.\n- **GlobalFoundries — $375 million** for a U.S. foundry supporting multiple quantum *modalities* (the different physical recipes for building a qubit — see below).\n- **$100 million each** to Atom Computing, D-Wave, Infleqtion, PsiQuantum, Quantinuum, and Rigetti — a deliberate spread across the competing ways to physically build a qubit: superconducting circuits, neutral atoms, photons (individual particles of light), and trapped ions (single charged atoms pinned in place by electromagnetic fields).\n- **Diraq — up to $38 million** for silicon spin qubits (which encode a qubit in the magnetic \"spin\" of a single electron inside silicon — appealing because it could ride on existing chip-manufacturing lines)."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Why the U.S. Treasury just stopped pretending this is private-sector business","text":"Notice the strategy. They didn't pick one horse. They funded the foundry layer heavily (IBM, GlobalFoundries) because manufacturing capacity is the constraint you can't conjure with a clever idea, and then they bought options across every qubit technology that might win. That's not how you bet on something a decade away. That's how you bet on something you think is about to matter, when you don't yet know which implementation crosses the line first."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Not all \"quantum\" is the same — and the loudest one is the least proven","text":"Here's a distinction that gets flattened in almost every headline, including, if I'm honest, most of this post so far. \"Quantum\" isn't one technology. It's at least three, and they're at radically different stages of maturity. Lumping them together is how people end up either wildly overhyped or unfairly cynical. The $2 billion the government just spent is almost entirely on **computing** — the hardest, least mature, highest-ceiling pillar. But it's not the only one, and arguably not the one solving real problems *today*."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Not all \"quantum\" is the same — and the loudest one is the least proven","text":"**Quantum sensing is the pillar that's already working, and nobody talks about it.** Sensing uses the extreme delicacy of quantum states to *measure* the world rather than to compute — the same fragility that's a curse for computing becomes the whole point. This is the unglamorous one — atomic clocks (timekeepers so precise they'd drift less than a second over the age of the universe), magnetometers (ultra-sensitive detectors of magnetic fields), gravimeters (instruments that sense minuscule changes in gravity) — and it's the most commercially real of the three by a wide margin. Atomic clocks sit at *technology-readiness levels* 7–8 (a NASA-origin 1-to-9 scale for how close something is to real-world use; 7–8 means \"basically field-ready\"), already flying in GPS satellites and running in telecom networks; Infleqtion put an optical-clock product on a Royal Navy submarine in 2025. Q-CTRL demonstrated quantum sensors doing GPS-denied navigation **50× better** than the best conventional alternative — and called it, fairly, the first *true commercial* quantum advantage. No error correction, no logical qubits, no decade-long roadmap. It ships, it solves a problem someone is paying real money to solve, and it works now. It's also tiny in dollar terms — roughly $470 million of a ~$1.9 billion quantum market in 2025 — precisely because it's not where the hype capital goes."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Not all \"quantum\" is the same — and the loudest one is the least proven","text":"**Quantum communication and cryptography is real but niche — and the punchline is that the best defense isn't quantum at all.** Quantum Key Distribution (QKD — using quantum physics to share secret encryption keys in a way that physically exposes any eavesdropper, because the act of intercepting a quantum signal disturbs it) has been deployed in production networks since 2007; it works, it's been run continuously over commercial metro fiber for years. But it's expensive, distance-limited, and needs special hardware, so it's stayed confined to governments and critical infrastructure. Meanwhile the actual answer to the Q-Day encryption threat that's driving all the national-security money turns out to be **post-quantum cryptography (PQC)** — new mathematical algorithms that run on the classical computers we already own, now standardized by NIST. Sit with that irony for a second: the most urgent quantum *threat* is best defended with classical software, not quantum hardware. For most organizations — including, very directly, telecoms — \"going quantum-safe\" means deploying PQC this decade, not buying a quantum computer. That's a real problem with a real, near-term, mostly-classical solution."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"Not all \"quantum\" is the same — and the loudest one is the least proven","text":"**Quantum computing is the moonshot — the biggest prize, the longest fuse, and the one most at risk of being a solution hunting for a problem.** It pulls roughly 90% of all quantum investment, yet the U.S. Department of Defense's own mid-2025 assessment puts practical machines 10+ years out. Both can be true: the ceiling is enormous (it's the half of the flywheel I've spent this whole post on) *and* outside a few domains — quantum chemistry, materials simulation, certain optimization and cryptography-relevant problems — a lot of the proposed applications are still, candidly, looking for a problem that actually needs a quantum computer rather than a better classical algorithm or a GPU. The honest framing is that computing has the highest expected value and the widest error bars. Sensing is a sure, small thing today. Computing is a maybe-enormous thing later. They deserve completely different mental models, and completely different patience.\n\nThe reason I still spend most of my attention on the computing pillar is the flywheel — sensing and communication advance largely on their own clocks, but *computing* is the one locked in the accelerating loop with AI, which is exactly why its long fuse may burn faster than the DoD's ten-year estimate suggests."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The trajectory","text":"Put the milestones on one axis and the shape jumps out at you.\n\n  Figure: The gap between breakthroughs is shrinking. The solid line is what's happened; the dashed line is IBM's published roadmap.\n\nThe dashed half isn't my speculation — it's [IBM's own roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc): quantum advantage demonstrated by the end of 2026, a *fault-tolerant* system called **Starling** in **2029** — fault tolerance being the threshold where the machine keeps computing correctly even though its individual qubits keep erroring, the point at which quantum computers stop being lab curiosities and become dependable — with 200 logical qubits capable of 100 million error-corrected operations, 1,000 logical qubits in the early 2030s, and a 100,000-qubit quantum-centric supercomputer by **2033**. Roadmaps slip — I'd bet real money some of these dates do. But here's the thing about the AI timeline that everyone got wrong: it didn't slip late, it slipped *early*. The aggressive forecasts were the accurate ones. When a field crosses from \"can we?\" to \"how fast can we scale?\", the binding constraint becomes capital and manufacturing, and both of those just got a $2 billion injection with the U.S. government's name on the cap table."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The trajectory","text":"My actual prediction, stated plainly so you can hold me to it: **the first genuinely useful, error-corrected quantum computation — one that does something a classical machine practically cannot, in chemistry or materials — lands before 2030, probably closer to 2028.** And the reason it'll be early rather than late is the flywheel. Every AI improvement in error decoding pulls the fault-tolerance date forward; every quantum result that generates clean physical data pulls the next AI capability forward. The two timelines aren't independent. They're multiplying."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"What it means for the money","text":"A few honest caveats before the take. I work in AT&T's Chief Data Office, I'm naming companies including my own employer below, and none of this is investment advice — it's a framework, and quantum stocks in particular are a graveyard of great narratives and brutal drawdowns. With that on the table, here's how I'd think about the layers.\n\n**The foundation labs (Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, DeepMind) get *more* central, not less.** The instinct is to think quantum is a threat to the AI incumbents. It's the opposite. The organizations that own the best AI are the ones turning quantum from hardware into capability — AlphaQubit and Quantum Echoes both came out of Google. The lab that pairs frontier AI with quantum R&D under one roof has a compounding advantage nobody else can buy. If you believe in the flywheel, you believe the integrated labs widen their lead."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"What it means for the money","text":"**The giants split into two camps: those who own a layer of the loop, and those who rent it.** IBM just became something genuinely interesting again — not because of quantum hype, but because the government effectively underwrote it as the *foundry* for an entire industry, the way TSMC anchors classical chips. Owning manufacturing capacity for a scarce, strategic technology is a structurally good place to stand. Microsoft (betting on *topological* qubits — a still-largely-experimental design meant to be far more error-resistant from the ground up — plus the Azure cloud layer that will rent quantum access as a service) and NVIDIA (the NVQLink interconnect that makes every quantum machine need GPUs alongside it) are positioned to monetize the loop regardless of which qubit wins. The companies to watch warily are the ones with no position in either AI or quantum that will simply pay the toll — and that includes a lot of the market.\n\n**On my own industry:** I'd put telecoms like AT&T in the \"rent it, but with a real reason to care early\" bucket. The Q-Day encryption threat lands directly on anyone running critical network infrastructure, which makes post-quantum cryptography a near-term operational problem, not a 2030 curiosity. The defensive spend starts before the offensive capability arrives. That's worth sitting with."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"What it means for the money","text":"**The pure-play small caps (D-Wave, Rigetti, IonQ, Quantinuum, and the rest) are the high-variance leg.** The government just validated several of them with capital *and* equity, which de-risks the \"will they survive\" question and re-rates the sector. But a spread of $100M bets across competing technologies is, by design, an admission that most of them won't be the winner. Treat them as a basket of options, not convictions. The CHIPS money is a floor under the sector, not a guarantee for any single name."},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The part I'm least sure about — and why I'm posting anyway","text":"I'll be honest about what could break this thesis. Roadmaps slip; \"fault tolerant by 2029\" could mean 2032. Verifiable quantum advantage on a benchmark is not the same as commercial value, and the gap between them is exactly the gap AI is living in right now — we built the capability years before we figured out how to capture its worth. And government equity stakes in frontier tech are an experiment with its own failure modes; \"the U.S. became a quantum investor\" is a sentence that could age into a case study either direction.\n\nBut none of those undercut the core observation, which is the one I think most people are still missing: **these are no longer two separate bets.** AI and quantum have entered a loop where each one shortens the other's timeline, and the institutional capital — all the way up to the Treasury — has started moving as if that's true. The pattern is the same one we just lived through with AI: the curve looked gentle right up until the year it didn't. I'd rather be early and slightly wrong on the date than on time and completely unprepared for the shape.\n\nThe flywheel is spinning up. The only real question is how fast — and that's a question about how seriously you take a curve, which is the same skill that separated the people who saw AI coming from the people who got surprised by it.\n\n---"},{"source":"Post \"The flywheel is spinning up: quantum, AI, and the convergence nobody priced in\" (June 4, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/04/the-flywheel-is-spinning-up.html","heading":"The part I'm least sure about — and why I'm posting anyway","text":"*David Morgan is a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office, where he builds production AI systems and orchestration platforms. This post is analysis, not investment advice. More writing at [djmorgan26.github.io](https://djmorgan26.github.io); code at [github.com/djmorgan26](https://github.com/djmorgan26).*"},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"","text":"I've tried auto-routing in every major harness I use regularly. Cursor, Copilot, Windsurf, Claude Code. The result is the same every time: it picks wrong. Not occasionally — consistently, in both directions. Send it a complex multi-file refactor and it reaches for a fast cheap model. Ask it a simple factual question and it fires up the most capable model in the pool. The task complexity and the model selection have no reliable relationship.\n\nAt first I thought I was doing something wrong. Then I thought it was a specific harness problem. Now I think it's a category problem, and I think it's worth being honest about it, because every vendor is shipping this feature as if it works and most users have no way to know that it doesn't.","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"What \"auto\" actually means right now","text":"The Copilot case is the best-documented, because it leaked into the open. As of February 2026, Copilot's Auto mode was explicitly routing based on server availability, model health, and billing compliance — not the nature of the task. VS Magazine got GitHub on record: the system's \"immediate goal\" was to \"manage capacity\" and \"reduce the likelihood of rate limits.\" Task-aware routing was on the roadmap, planned since September 2025, and didn't make it to a partial VS Code release until May 2026 — eight months later. Right now, if you select Opus in the UI, the system may quietly route you to Sonnet if Opus is under load. Your model selection is treated as a preference signal, not an instruction. And Microsoft built in a 10% request discount for users who stay in Auto mode — which is a load-balancing subsidy dressed up as a product benefit.\n\nThe gap between the September 2025 promise and the May 2026 partial delivery is itself the answer to how hard task-aware routing actually is."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"It's not only Copilot","text":"I use Copilot as the example because the details are documented. But the problem is universal.\n\nLiteLLM, which is one of the infrastructure-layer routing solutions people reach for when they want to roll their own, explicitly doesn't do task classification. It routes to the cheapest deployment that satisfies the infrastructure constraints — cost per token, latency threshold, provider availability. That's useful, but it's not intelligence. OpenRouter as of this month has 341 text models in its catalog. What it doesn't have is a reliable mechanism for matching a specific task to the right one out of 341. It's a gateway. Cursor's \"auto\" mode will tell you it picks the right model for your task. My experience is that it picks a model, and then tells you it was the right one.\n\nThe pattern holds everywhere: every harness ships the feature checkbox before solving the underlying problem. The checkbox is table stakes now — you can't ship a developer tool without some story about automatic model selection. So they all ship a story. Most of it is availability management with a better name."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"Why this is genuinely hard","text":"There's a reason nobody has solved this yet, and it's not lack of effort.\n\nTask classification at inference time in a production environment is a legitimately difficult problem. To do it well, you need ground-truth quality labels on outputs — some signal that tells you whether the model that was selected actually handled the task well. You don't get that by default. In a controlled eval, you can measure output quality against a benchmark. In production, your user sends a prompt, gets a response, and may or may not do anything that tells you whether the response was good. Most interactions leave no useful quality signal behind.\n\nThe research results look promising on paper. RouteLLM published at ICLR 2025 claims 85% cost savings at 95% GPT-4 quality on MT Bench. Those numbers are real in the experimental context — a fixed benchmark, known answer distributions, clean evaluation criteria. Your real workload isn't MT Bench. Your real workload is a mix of tasks you didn't anticipate, edge cases your classifier wasn't trained on, prompts that fall between categories. The benchmark transfers imperfectly to production, and the parts where it fails to transfer are exactly the parts that erode trust."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"Why this is genuinely hard","text":"The other piece is that model capabilities change constantly. A routing classifier trained on the model pool from Q4 2025 has a different ground truth than the same classifier running in Q2 2026. Every major model release — and we've had several this year — shifts which tasks route where. A classifier that isn't retrained on the new capability landscape makes systematic errors, and users experience those as \"the auto-routing always picks wrong.\""},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"The reason for optimism — and why you can't feel it yet","text":"Here's what I actually believe: the feedback loop is forming. It just hasn't closed yet.\n\nEvery Auto-mode session is, implicitly, a data collection event. What task came in, which model got selected, what the output was, whether the user accepted it or iterated. That's a training signal. Every major harness vendor has access to it at scale. The labs know what good routing data looks like, they're accumulating it, and the models trained on it will produce better classifiers than anything trained on public benchmarks.\n\nBut that flywheel has only been spinning for a year or so at the kind of scale that would matter. The models trained on that data haven't made it to production in force. You can't feel a feedback loop that hasn't had time to close. The gap between \"we're collecting the right signal\" and \"users can actually feel the improvement\" is longer than the marketing cycle for AI features, so from the outside it looks like nothing is happening. Something is happening. It's just slower than the feature announcements would suggest.\n\nI expect this to be meaningfully better in twelve to eighteen months. That's not pessimism — that's how long these loops take to deliver."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"The trust problem is worse than the capability problem","text":"Here's what concerns me more than the technical gap: the trust deficit that builds while the technical gap closes.\n\nA harness that consistently picks the wrong model is one you stop relying on. Once you stop relying on Auto, you're back to manual selection — which means the feature is effectively useless for most users (who don't know which model to pick) and a workflow interruption for power users (who will override it anyway). The adoption path for auto-routing runs through trust, and trust is being borrowed against a capability that isn't there yet.\n\nThe Copilot frustration for non-Microsoft tasks probably has a routing explanation. If the Auto pool skews toward whatever model is available, and if the training signal skews toward tasks that are common on Microsoft platforms, users doing work that doesn't match that distribution get worse routing. Not worse models — worse selection. And they experience it as the tool not working, which isn't exactly wrong, but also isn't the right diagnosis."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"The trust problem is worse than the capability problem","text":"The fix — transparency — is underrated. Right now, when Auto picks wrong, you don't know it picked wrong until you see a bad output. If the harness showed you the routing decision (\"this request was classified as a short, low-complexity task and routed to GPT-4.1 mini\"), you could develop intuition about when to trust it. You could also catch systematic misclassification quickly and override it intelligently. Without that visibility, every bad output is just a mystery, and the auto-routing feature takes the blame regardless of whether the routing was the problem."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"Why this matters beyond model selection","text":"I keep coming back to the fact that routing is not a convenience feature. It's infrastructure.\n\nWithout reliable routing, token budgets are essentially arbitrary. You can set limits on what an agent can spend, but you can't calibrate those limits to actual task complexity if you don't know whether a given task warranted a $0.003 call or a $0.03 one. You can't build a cost model for agents running at any real scale. You can't report productivity-per-token back to a team in any way that would help them make better decisions. The whole economics of running AI at scale run through routing, and right now the routing layer isn't solid enough to put weight on.\n\nThe harnesses that figure this out first won't just have a better product feature. They'll have the infrastructure to make AI cost-effective in organizations that are genuinely trying to run it at scale, and that's a much larger prize than a checkbox in the settings menu."},{"source":"Post \"Auto doesn't mean smart\" (June 19, 2026)","url":"/2026/06/19/auto-doesnt-mean-smart.html","heading":"Where this ends up","text":"I think auto-routing will work. The data exists, the research is real, and the vendors have every incentive to close this gap. A year from now, the feedback loops that are forming today will have had time to actually run, and the classifiers trained on real production data will be substantially better than what's shipping now.\n\nBut right now, in June 2026, every harness offers auto-routing and none of them consistently delivers on the promise. The feature looks like table stakes. The implementation is mostly load management. Don't rely on it for work where model selection actually matters. Pick your model deliberately. Know why. Check back in a year — by then, it should have earned the trust it's currently being extended by default.\n\n---\n\n*David Morgan is a Senior Data/AI Engineer at AT&T's Chief Data Office, where he builds production AI systems and orchestration platforms. More writing at [djmorgan26.github.io](https://djmorgan26.github.io); code at [github.com/djmorgan26](https://github.com/djmorgan26).*"},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"","text":"*The views here are my own and don't represent my employer.*\n\n[Download the white paper (PDF)](/assets/papers/developing-at-enterprise-scale.pdf)","lead":true},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Executive summary","text":"This paper shows what it really looks like to be a developer inside a large enterprise in the age of AI, right now. Not the demo version. The version where you own a project from the first requirement to the last production change, inside a company that's built for safety and scale, not for agents.\n\nThe tagline I keep coming back to is that everyone is a developer now. If you're not a developer and you aren't using AI, you're already behind. If you were a developer before AI and haven't adopted it, you may be behind too. That's the hook. The point is what the work actually looks like day to day.\n\nI've worked as a solo developer and on teams of very different sizes, on very different projects. They all end up the same way: I'm responsible for every facet of the project. In this paper I walk through that whole lifecycle from the seat of a senior developer, starting the moment someone hands you an idea and ending in production and beyond.\n\nMy own company is a fair middle case. It's ahead of the game compared with large enterprises that have been big for a long time, and most likely behind the tech startups. So this isn't a complaint about one company. It's what the job realistically looks like when you can move fast and the organization around you has good reasons to be careful."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Executive summary","text":"The short version is that AI lets one person move very fast inside a company that moves more carefully. That speed doesn't come from typing code faster. It comes from planning harder, codifying what your company expects into skills your whole team shares, engineering the context your agents work from, and spending tokens up front so you don't spend far more of them later.\n\nThe friction is just as real. Governance, security gates, identity, networking and change windows don't go away, and they shouldn't: they protect customers, data and the business. But right now they can slow you down as much as AI speeds you up. Holding both of those truths at once is what being a developer looks like in 2026."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Who this is for","text":"I'm writing this for someone who wants a software job or is curious about one. It's also for people who already have a software job and want to know what top talent thinks about when starting new projects, and how things really work at a big company.\n\nA lot of people don't know what coders do now that AI helps. They think it's all AI. It's not. And if you're a developer outside a big company, you can skip many of these steps; a lot of them exist because of enterprise scale."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The job at a glance","text":"Figure: The twelve areas this paper covers. You move between them and loop back as things change, with skills, context engineering and governance running through all of them."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The fast developer at the slow company","text":"Picture a senior developer at a large enterprise. The company is trying to move fast, and for its size it does, but it still moves slower than you can. You, with AI at your side, are in a unique position to move really fast inside it. That gap between your speed and the company's speed shapes almost everything in this paper.\n\nI'm starting from a simple assumption: there's already an idea, or someone has created a project and handed it to you. You didn't pick the problem. Now it's yours, from the first requirement to the last production change.\n\nAnd it really is all yours. Over the life of one project you'll be the developer, the architect, the project manager, the DevOps engineer, the tester and the site reliability engineer. There's no one person who should just be doing one thing anymore. AI is what makes that possible, and it's also what makes it expected.\n\nHere are the areas I'll walk through. They're roughly in the order you'll first run into them, but this is agile, not waterfall, so you'll loop back through most of them more than once:"},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The fast developer at the slow company","text":"1. Planning and requirements\n2. Managing change, because this is agile and not waterfall\n3. Coding with skills, plugins and connectors\n4. Testing\n5. Environments and CI/CD\n6. Observability\n7. Enterprise networking and identity\n8. Realigning your docs and tests with reality\n9. Onboarding clients and listening to telemetry\n10. Deadlines and security gates\n11. Governance and life in production\n12. What comes after: your career, your token budget and your tools"},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Know where you are on the AI fluency spectrum","text":"AI fluency is a spectrum, and it's important to know where you are on it. Most people at an enterprise are still making their first vibe-coded HTML pages or slides. That's a fine place to start, but it's only the start.\n\nDon't be the person who presents a \"cool\" AI-generated HTML report, sends it to the whole org, and explains that if AI could do this, everyone should be able to. If you can't recognize slop, you aren't experienced enough with AI yet."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"1. Planning and requirements","text":"It all starts with the plan. Once you have your project idea, you hopefully have a set of requirements. There's a really good chance you don't, so you'll be working with a team of people to create them."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Documents first, and they live where your company keeps them","text":"You may want a product requirements document, a business requirements document and an architecture diagram. It's not that simple at a big company, because things work in specific ways there. You'll need to leverage skills, plugins and connectors to do it right.\n\nThe material you need is scattered. Some of it sits in Confluence or a wiki, some in SharePoint, some in databases you have to request access to. Some of those places your AI can reach and some it can't, and you need to know which before you count on it."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Architecture diagrams from approved patterns","text":"You can't just draw any architecture diagram. You need to follow your company's approved patterns. My approach is to turn those patterns, which are often draw.io files as well as Markdown templates, into a skill. The AI then uses the skill to produce a diagram that fits my project.\n\nOften you need more than one: a technical diagram and an overview diagram, because different teams ask for different things. You don't want to recreate these by hand every time, which is exactly why it belongs in a skill."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Requirements are the hardest part","text":"This is the trickiest part of working fast at a slow company. People don't understand how robust requirements need to be in the age of AI, and they don't really know how to get there. Whether you use Jira or GitHub Issues, your job is to discover every gap you can.\n\nThis is where it's really powerful to be someone who understands the business, understands the domain and understands the technical side of the solution. You might have a skill that breaks everything you know, and every source you can reach, into features, epics, user stories, requirements and acceptance criteria. Use whatever structure you and your team or company decide is best. Either way, it comes down to you.\n\nYou need to know what the user expects. You need to know your SLAs and your SLOs. These aren't hypotheticals; they're hard caps. If there's conditional logic in a requirement, like \"if this is possible, then do that,\" figure it out before you build, not during.\n\nThink about who will use what you build, too. Every site or app today should be designed for how humans will interact with it and how agents will interact with it, not just one or the other."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Check the artifact repository before you write a line","text":"If your project depends on specific packages, you'd better know how your company's artifact repository works, whether that's JFrog Artifactory or something else. Check it to confirm your literal technical requirements are actually available.\n\nHere's how that goes wrong. Say your requirements call for a specific version of a library or runtime. You plan around it, design around it and start coding. Then you find out that version isn't in Artifactory. Now you have to use a different version, or a different tool, and it doesn't do the thing you planned on. Work you've already done has to change. The later you catch it, the more it costs.\n\nThat's why it's important to have skills that check what's available, and what's newest, in Artifactory, and to know how to check yourself. And if you need something that isn't there, know how to request it. That can mean a software approval for the package, license or software, and approvals take time, so start early."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Get your team on the same page","text":"The same goes for the people on your team. Get on the same page about what they're comfortable with. If they're not comfortable with the thing you want to use, work it out now.\n\nContainerize as much as possible, with Docker Desktop or Podman. Put everything in a container.\n\nCodify everything else too. Use Terraform for your infrastructure as code, or Helm if you're leveraging Kubernetes. When your containers, infrastructure and configuration are all code, they're easy to parameterize per environment and easy to run through your pipelines.\n\nDon't write your automation as Mac-only scripts, bash scripts or PowerShell scripts. Use something operating-system agnostic. That usually means Python: it runs on Windows and Mac, and AI is really good at writing it. If you have stricter performance requirements, you might choose something else; it depends on what you're building.\n\nThe point is to make your work friendly to everyone around you. You don't want to be the only one who can work on it, and you don't want to ship things that don't work on other people's computers."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"2. Managing change: this is agile, not waterfall","text":"Now you're done with planning. Probably. Maybe. Hopefully. But this isn't waterfall; we don't work in waterfall anymore. Even a software factory tends to push you back toward waterfall, and that just doesn't really work. I've tried it. Requirements change all the time, and things get really messy.\n\nSo make sure you have a skill that helps manage the project, a skill that acts as a project manager. Honestly, you'd love an actual project manager who knows how to use that skill. You don't want to be the developer who has to solve all of it.\n\nIf you are in that position, it's still possible. It's just a lot more work. Every time a new requirement arrives, because someone changed their mind or because I realized something I hadn't before (which is bound to happen), I don't want to stop and work out what I now have to do before I can do it. I just want to do what I'm told. Life isn't that easy, and that's the truth. A good project-management skill is how you close some of that gap."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"3. Coding with skills, plugins and connectors","text":"Now you start coding. You have your requirements, your plans and your design docs. To stay on the same page as everyone else, and to work the way a large company works, you should be using skills, plugins and connectors here too, not just in planning."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Capability skills and enterprise preference skills","text":"I break skills into two kinds. Capability skills teach your agent how to do something. Preference skills teach it how things are done at your company: the things anyone working there simply has to know.\n\nThe proxy is a big example. Large companies need very secure environments, so you have to understand how the network is configured and what you need to do on your side to integrate with it correctly. That might mean creating private links or DNS entries. You'd want skills for all of that."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Share skills across every harness with a package manager","text":"Those skills also need to work across different harnesses, IDEs and agents. That takes something standardized. Microsoft's Agent Package Manager (APM) is really good for this. APM supports multiple clients, so even if your team uses different harnesses and tools, everyone gets the same experience. Another really good thing about it is that you can pull from other people's marketplaces and registries, not just your own.\n\nYou could keep a local APM repo, and that does work, but your company can't track it. You probably want a private enterprise repo instead, set up however your company mandates. Your company is likely trying to look at everything you do and learn from it: how you use AI, how the company is leveraging AI, whether you're good at coding and at getting work done. If they want that, you listen and work with it. The upside is that you can share exactly what your team needs."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Structure your repos early","text":"Now you're working across several repositories: the APM repo, your application repo, and maybe an infrastructure repo. Maybe you have a DevOps person. That's an extremely important role on a project. If you don't know DevOps, either leverage skills for it or find someone who does. At some point you'll be all of these roles, but you can't always start from zero (even though sometimes you have to).\n\nI also like one repo that contains all the others, a kind of context repo, whatever people call it. I use Git submodules for this, which gets a little messy with the submodule pointers.\n\nYour documentation changes all the time, so automate keeping it current. You can let your agent manage it locally while you work, or run dedicated clean-up sessions. Another strategy is to use GitHub Actions or Azure DevOps pipelines with cloud agents, such as GitHub Copilot's cloud agent, to read your current code and update the docs, perhaps on every change to your main branch. This gets messy fast, so set up the structure early, so that your workflow scales with your skills and your codebase."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Context engineering is the most important thing","text":"Think about your hosting before you build. Maybe you're on Azure, AWS or Google Cloud. Maybe you're on-premises, or hybrid. You want skills, or at least a connection to the right documentation, for whichever it is.\n\nYour agents are super smart, but you can waste a lot of time by not spending the extra tokens to give them the extra context. With it, the agent can get things done the first time and the right way. It's still a bit of a luck of the draw. But context engineering is on your side, and it's the most important thing for getting all of this done.\n\nBy now you have your skills and your repos, your whole team can use them, and it's all containerized. Now you're really coding."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The biggest thing: let your AI verify its own work","text":"Giving your AI the ability to verify is the biggest thing. For a UI, that might be Playwright or Puppeteer, so the agent can open the app and see what it built. For almost anything, it's telemetry. Whatever it is, the AI should be able to get the context it needs, when it needs it, and check its own work instead of guessing.\n\nThat's where progressive disclosure comes in. Instead of loading everything into context up front, you give the agent a short map and let it pull in the detail it needs as it goes. It's one of the context-engineering techniques every worker in the age of AI needs to understand, not just developers."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Make your agent's answers readable by humans","text":"Make sure that when your AI responds to you or to others, it doesn't just cite requirement IDs, or IDs in general. It should make things digestible without all the references. I don't want to click on links just to understand what my agent is trying to tell me.\n\nThis is one way to bridge the gap between an effective project structure and the humans who are still part of the development process. Most enterprise work hasn't reached human-out-of-the-loop yet. So we need to understand what's happening most of the time, even those of us who trust AI more than others do."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"4. Testing","text":"When you're building an application, you need to think about testing. Early on, though, you may not want to think about it too much. You'd just be creating more things you have to go back and change as the application matures, and early on you're only about halfway there."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Right-size it early, and parameterize everything","text":"Write the essential tests while you work. Maybe you practice test-driven development; that's up to you. Don't overdo it, but do make sure your tests are parameterized and scalable, and that you can run them locally.\n\nParameterization is what lets you move from environment to environment. At an enterprise you might have two environments or three: dev, stage and prod, or a dedicated test environment. You might need simulated clients too. If you're building the server and client VMs will connect to it, you want to test exactly how that's going to work."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Enterprise scans and automated reviews","text":"You also need the scans enterprises run, whether that's SonarQube, CodeQL or the many others I could name. You're testing for code smells, for linting (with tools like Ruff), for all of it, so that you know you're writing good code.\n\nOver time you might add automated GitHub Copilot reviews on every commit, push or pull request into main. Set it up how you like. Companies care a lot more about security now than they used to."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Testing means the whole range","text":"When I say testing, I don't just mean unit tests. I mean unit, end-to-end, integration, penetration, load and chaos testing, and more. What you need depends on your language and the kind of application you're building.\n\nYour agents can now spin up isolated containers, act as an adversary and try to break in. Use strategies like that to prove your product meets the requirements you set at the beginning, or the ones you discovered along the way. Maybe you learned you need a guaranteed P99 latency, or that the application must stay up 99.9999% of the time. The only way to really know is to test for all of it, including the cases your agent thinks of that you forgot to expect.\n\nSo even though you're \"just\" a software developer, you're also the tester. You're the site reliability engineer, the DevOps engineer and the project manager. You're all of these things."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"5. Environments and CI/CD","text":"Let's say you've used every skill and tool you have to build the application. You've done the easy part, maybe 80% of what it really takes."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Show it early and fail fast","text":"Now you need to show it to your clients. This is a fail-fast environment: if you aren't failing fast and showing people what you have, you'll wear your clients out. Showing them early lets them guide you while you build. That's why it's agile.\n\nYou start making real progress. The application seems close to ready in a dev environment. Then you need to move it to a higher environment. Maybe you set that up early, maybe you didn't, and either way it takes a lot of work."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Read your stakeholders","text":"When you show your work, learn to tell your stakeholders apart. Some are perfectionists. Others are there to solve a problem and care far more about whether it works. Some value things more when they feel authentic, and some like them more when they look cool.\n\nMany people can now tell when something was AI-generated, or even when it was tweaked after AI generated it. I'm not the perfectionist here myself, but knowing your audience mattered before AI and it will keep mattering."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Connect your agent to the real environment","text":"Make sure your agent has CLIs, APIs and MCP servers that connect to the actual environment. They bridge the gap between what your application does and what that environment needs."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"A promote workflow, never a shortcut to prod","text":"You need CI/CD. The flow I aim for looks like this:\n\n1. Pass CI locally.\n2. Push your branch and open a pull request, which runs the automated checks.\n3. Pass CI on the pull request.\n4. Build one image and push it to your artifact repository, such as Artifactory.\n5. Let your pipelines pull that same image and deploy it to the right environment.\n6. Promote it from environment to environment. You never pull directly into prod.\n\nYou have to think through all of this just to get your application into the environment you want, safely and in the right order."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Build yourself a diagnostic panel","text":"It's really helpful to build diagnostic panels that only admins can see. They tell you which environment you're in, how it's performing and anything else you need at a glance."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"6. Observability","text":"It's really important to capture logs, metrics and traces, and maybe more. You need all three, and you need them correlated through traces, so that you know what happened whenever anyone does anything. If there's an issue, you find it.\n\nOne way to do this is Prometheus, Loki and Tempo to capture metrics, logs and traces, with Grafana to show them in dashboards. The dashboards are mostly for you. Your agent can query Prometheus directly, which is super useful. That's just one stack; many other providers can give you logs, metrics and traces.\n\nObservability lines up with your testing. It also enables a kind of interactive testing, where your agent uses the application, looks at all the telemetry and confirms it works."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Decide where telemetry lives and for how long","text":"You also need to decide how long you keep telemetry and where. Is it in Azure Storage? A Log Analytics workspace? Is this a SOX application? If it is, you're required to retain it for 13 months. These are the things you actually have to think about in an enterprise setting."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"7. Enterprise networking and identity","text":"Then there's everything that makes enterprise infrastructure enterprise: private endpoints, DNS records, corporate DNS versus private DNS, and the SLOs and wait times on every request you make to other teams. How do those requests get approved? Do you approve them yourself? Do other admins have to? Are any of the approvals automated?\n\nMaybe this is all captured in skills, because someone at your company did a good job and wrote it down. Or maybe you have to figure it out on your own. If you do, don't keep it to yourself. Share it."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Identity is where automation meets the wall","text":"Identity bites hardest when you start onboarding people. Say you need groups for access. You could create them yourself from the command line, but that's not really good enough. You're supposed to use an internal application your company built to make group creation easier.\n\nExcept you can't automate that application, unless you use Playwright. Then you need an interactive sign-in, because everything goes through the company's single sign-on. That's deliberately hard to automate, because your company isn't built for agents. It's built for safety and enterprise scale. So you either figure out how to automate it with Playwright, or you go create the groups by hand.\n\nThen a new platform arrives that manages all the groups. Even if you made it through the old process, now you have to onboard to the new one. Maybe the team behind it has to create the groups for you."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Decide what you want to own","text":"This raises a question you have to answer deliberately: do I create and manage these things, or does the client? Think about the lifetime of the project. As it scales out, do you want to be responsible for all these little things, or should they be someone else's responsibility?"},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"8. Realigning docs and tests with reality","text":"If you got this far, you're probably pretty far into your project. You probably think the application is pretty good. There might be a few bugs; you might know what they are, or you might not.\n\nYou've also noticed that you have an insane amount of tests and an insane amount of documentation. Once again, you need to go back and update a lot of it to match the current reality of the project, because the project has shifted in big ways. Everything needs to be aligned.\n\nAs much as you don't want to spend tokens, and as much as you want to stretch your company's token allocation, this step is essential. Please do it. The more out of date and misaligned your context is, the worse time you'll have, and the more tokens you'll spend later. Spend them up front."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"9. Onboarding clients and listening to telemetry","text":"Now people are onboarded, and you're relying on telemetry to see how they're doing. Some feedback comes in: this looks good, this doesn't work, this throws an error we don't catch.\n\nBetter still, you catch some of that yourself before anyone tells you, because you set up the right telemetry. It's never going to be perfect, but it gives you a real shot."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"10. Tight deadlines and noisy security gates","text":"Now you're trying to meet deadlines that are tight, sometimes tighter than they should be. That's partly because the company expects AI to let fewer people move faster, and in a lot of ways it does. What hasn't caught up yet is an understanding of what all the extra checks cost.\n\nThe more security checks you face and the more skills you're handed, the more they partially help and partially hurt. If the skills aren't really high quality, they slow you down. You might be ten times as productive, maybe a hundred times, and still be going back to change things constantly.\n\nOne change triggers one minor code smell. That means I redo my tests. I thought I was about to push good code through my pipeline, and instead I'm redoing all of it. Meanwhile, reports flag you as red and tell you to turn green, to be more secure or to attest that you did something, without giving you exact instructions or a skill for how.\n\nNone of these checks is unreasonable on its own; most exist because something went wrong somewhere before. But until they're codified into high-quality skills, blueprints and best practices, everything stays messy."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"10. Tight deadlines and noisy security gates","text":"How you build those skills matters too. Making them dynamic, so they reference your docs instead of copying them, means fewer things to update when a doc changes. But if a doc moves, you have more to change. That's why upfront structure and planning are essential: they make things change less often. Usually more dynamic is the right answer, but too many references can get annoying.\n\nSkills also need to be maintained. Tools change all the time at big companies, and communication about those changes is pretty poor. A skill that was right when you wrote it may not be right today."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"11. Governance and life in production","text":"You're finally there. You have a good application with real reliability engineering behind it, and you're ready to move to production. Maybe you went through pre-production first, running in the production environment with real people in it but not technically in production yet."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The governance gauntlet","text":"Now you have to make sure you went through governance correctly. If you're building an AI product, governance might be fast-tracked when a senior leader is sponsoring it. Or you might wait six months or longer, because the reviews are thorough and a request passes through a lot of hands.\n\nThe checklist before you can even build is long. Typically it includes:\n\n- Governance approvals for each stage of the app, from a build approval through an operating approval\n- The enterprise licenses you need\n- Software approvals for the additional packages, licenses or software you want to use\n- SOX approval, which is rigorous, as it should be\n- AI approval\n- Subscription approval, then onboarding to that subscription\n\nThen, to actually go to production, you submit to governance again, so everyone knows how you work and can confirm you pass all the checks."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The governance gauntlet","text":"And that's not all. You also have to do app creation so a budget can be tied to your application. That's partly politics, different ways to split the imaginary dollar, and partly how a company this size keeps track of where its money goes. You have to pick a production release date before you even start the project, too. The business needs a date to plan around, so you're forced to guess, and in my experience the first guess is almost always wrong. That's why expectations kill: set them carefully and update them early. Depending on the project and its requirements, you may need firewall requests and network exceptions as well. There's more, but you get the gist: approvals upon approvals, each one there to manage a real risk."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Production is a new set of constraints","text":"OK, great, you're in production. Now you monitor everything and figure out how to safely get new code in. You'll probably need a change request for anything that touches production. Then you hit a window where the company says no changes in production. So what do you do?\n\nNew vulnerabilities show up too. Your checks now catch them, and they come far more often, because you're checking far more things. You're safer, but you're moving slower. That's just how things work right now. It will get better over time, but there are a lot of rough patches today."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Your next project is bigger","text":"Then you finally finish. Maybe this is your life's project and you stay on it for years. More often, people who are good at figuring all of this out get a new assignment: \"Go do what you did over there, over here.\" They keep getting new and bigger opportunities.\n\nSo you work your way up, one project after another. Along the way you might do only some of these steps, but you take on more and more responsibility. You stop being a solo developer and start leading a team. At some point you start having your own ideas and following through on your own projects, assigning people to them instead of only developing someone else's idea.\n\nThe more you work, the more people notice, and the more you move up. But it's tough. You have to move really fast, and not everyone wants to speed up with you. Some would rather you slow down and be safer, and they have a point, but the pace isn't going back."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Tokens are a balancing act","text":"More tokens means more compute, and more compute means more productivity. Intelligence keeps getting smarter, and it keeps getting cheaper. So it's a balancing act.\n\nYou want to move fast, fast, fast. Think of it as a spectrum: agent swarms, a few agents in parallel, one agent at a time, or no agents at all. The further toward swarms I go at my company, the faster I'd run out of tokens. I'd be more productive, but I'd also be more wasteful. When a deliverable is about to miss its deadline, maybe I'd have to spend like that. Even then, a swarm usually isn't the safe choice in an enterprise setting. But it cuts both ways: the more productive and efficient you are with your tokens, the more tokens you may be given.\n\nTokens won't be an issue once model orchestrators get good enough. They're just starting to get decent, so hopefully that happens soon.\n\nUntil then, if you need to meet an important deadline, go all out. Prove you can do good work and move fast, and don't worry too much about the tokens. But have KPIs ready to prove you deserve more resources. If you're a developer, that could be your Git history. It could be demos, or many other kinds of evidence. Maybe your company tracks this for you; maybe you need to track it yourself."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"The tools and the terms are converging","text":"By the time I finish a project like this, I blink and my company has all these new tools I can use. They're all basically the same and interchangeable: different providers offering the same products. To be honest, Anthropic tends to come up with the good ideas and implement them first, and then the other companies see them and copy them.\n\nThe same goes for the vocabulary. AI agent, super agent, orchestration agent, superintelligence, plain old AI: the meanings behind most of these new terms are mostly the same. People just love creating new names. That's another thing that will always be true at big companies: new acronyms and new terms for the same thing."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Looking ahead to 2027","text":"That's the life of a developer inside an enterprise in 2026. 2027 will probably look very similar. I can't see further out than that.\n\nWe'll probably have software factories set up by then, where the person with the idea gets the money. And there will still be people who aren't good at using AI to code, because they're worried about it. They are often good at catching bad decisions, though, and they're good people to have around. You just don't want them right next to you while you build. You want them on the requirements side."},{"source":"Post \"Developing at Enterprise Scale: What the Job Really Looks Like in the Age of AI\" (November 18, 2026)","url":"/2026/11/18/developing-at-enterprise-scale.html","heading":"Conclusion","text":"That's what it really looks like to be a developer inside an enterprise right now. Everyone may be a developer now, but inside a big company the developer's job is every job on the project. AI makes one person fast enough to carry an idea from requirements to production. The company around that person is still slower than a startup, and it's slow for real reasons: security, compliance, identity and governance.\n\nThe developers who do well in this era don't fight that. They plan harder up front, codify what their company expects into skills their whole team shares, engineer the context their agents need, and spend tokens early so they don't waste them later. Then, when they figure something out, they share it."}];
